  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14235769/assets/063fafc4f8c50dba56bcfb50b8366a2ffb8c713f5668ce22d10c0370376bbaba.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-x=-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2+x=2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le 2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2z\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }2rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r[{z}^{2}]_{z=-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{z=2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r((2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r+4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{[2{r}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{r=0}^{r=3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2(3)^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}(3)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }18+36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[18\\theta +36\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi } \\\\  & =18(\\pi -0)+36(0-0) \\\\  & =18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
